---
import { getCollection, type CollectionEntry } from 'astro:content';
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import CaseDiagram from '../../components/CaseDiagram.astro';
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const entries = (await getCollection('work', ({ data }) => data.published)).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
  return entries.map((entry, index) => ({ params: { slug: entry.id }, props: { entry, prev: entries[index - 1], next: entries[index + 1] } }));
}
interface Props { entry: CollectionEntry<'work'>; prev?: CollectionEntry<'work'>; next?: CollectionEntry<'work'> }
const { entry, prev, next } = Astro.props;
const navEntry = (item?: CollectionEntry<'work'>) => item ? ({ id: item.id, title: item.data.title }) : undefined;
---
<CaseLayout title={entry.data.title} description={entry.data.summary} eyebrow={entry.data.eyebrow} status={entry.data.status} outcome={entry.data.outcome} prev={navEntry(prev)} next={navEntry(next)} variant={entry.id === 'memory-efficient-distribution' ? 'case-one' : entry.id === 'removing-redundant-work' ? 'case-two' : entry.id === 'shipping-new-format' ? 'case-three' : undefined}>
  {entry.id === 'memory-efficient-distribution' && <>
    <section class="case-section glance"><p class="section-label">At a glance</p><div class="glance-grid"><div><small>Problem</small><p>A producer-side OOM investigation exposed full-dataset materialization in both export and serving.</p></div><div><small>My role</small><p>End-to-end investigation, option evaluation, POC measurement, and proposed architecture design.</p></div><div><small>Current status</small><p>Direction supported by a POC; distribution, lifecycle, and failure semantics remain in review.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">The problem</p><h2>Full-dataset materialization appeared at more than one point in the data path.</h2><p>The exporter scanned the distributed store, built the dataset, and generated a serialized artifact. The serving layer later downloaded that artifact and materialized the complete dataset in memory again.</p><p>The operational incident was a producer-side OOM. Serving-side materialization had not caused an incident; it was an architectural limitation I identified by extending the investigation across the end-to-end flow.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section visual"><p class="section-label">Understanding the existing system</p><CaseDiagram type="distribution-existing" /></section>
    <section class="case-section prose options-section"><p class="section-label">Options considered</p><h2>The key decision was where lookups should happen.</h2><p>A Principal Engineer suggested LMDB as a candidate technology. I evaluated it alongside alternatives, considering serialization cost, memory behavior, artifact distribution, and the latency and capacity implications of remote access.</p><ol class="decision-list"><li><span>01</span><div><h3>Direct remote lookups</h3><p>Simpler local state, rejected because it would add network dependency, latency, and capacity demand to a latency-sensitive path.</p></div><em>Rejected</em></li><li><span>02</span><div><h3>Sharded serialized artifacts</h3><p>A viable incremental fallback with smaller distribution units, but serving would still materialize serialized data in memory.</p></div><em>Fallback</em></li><li class="selected"><span>03</span><div><h3>Local LMDB snapshot</h3><p>Keeps membership lookups local while changing the data representation and distribution model.</p></div><em>Selected direction</em></li></ol></section>
    <section class="case-section visual"><p class="section-label">Proposed architecture</p><CaseDiagram type="distribution-proposed" /></section>
    <section class="case-section validation"><div><p class="section-label">Validation</p><h2>POC evidence, not a production outcome.</h2><p>Using data representative of production scale, the POC tested membership-only lookups and storage efficiency. An 8-byte key representation was an experiment to measure the compaction trade-off, not a production decision.</p><span class="poc-stamp">Proof of concept · directional evidence</span></div><dl><div><dt>~27 B <i>→</i> 8 B</dt><dd>Average identifier/key representation in the experiment</dd></div><div><dt>~43%</dt><dd>POC LMDB footprint reduction</dd></div><div><dt>~31%</dt><dd>Compressed snapshot reduction</dd></div></dl></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Trade-offs</p><h2>Local lookups create a distribution problem that must be designed explicitly.</h2><p>Read-only local snapshots keep remote access outside the hot path and avoid materializing the complete dataset in application memory. In return, the system must define snapshot publication, distribution, activation, rollback, lifecycle, and failure behavior. Those semantics remain part of the architecture review.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose contribution"><p class="section-label">My contribution</p><h2>I connected the immediate failure to the wider architecture.</h2><p>I extended the investigation across producer and serving, evaluated alternatives, built and measured POCs, explored storage and key representations, and designed the proposed integration. LMDB was initially suggested by a Principal Engineer; my ownership was evaluating the direction and developing the supporting design and evidence. The architecture is not production-deployed.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section status-section"><p class="section-label">Current status</p><div><h2>POC-supported direction. Architecture review in progress.</h2><p>The POC supports the storage direction; snapshot distribution, activation, rollback, lifecycle, and failure semantics remain under review and refinement.</p></div></section>
  </>}
  {entry.id === 'removing-redundant-work' && <>
    <section class="case-section glance"><p class="section-label">At a glance</p><div class="glance-grid"><div><small>Problem</small><p>A high-volume path paid for sorted pagination without needing its additional guarantee.</p></div><div><small>My role</small><p>Investigation, equivalence validation, change, canary, and monitoring.</p></div><div><small>Result</small><p>Average production runtime fell from ~0.65s to ~0.15s after rollout.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">The problem</p><h2>The expensive step was hidden behind a reasonable abstraction.</h2><p>The processing path was slower than expected. The symptom pointed to a high-volume operation, but not yet to the work responsible for its runtime.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose investigation"><p class="section-label">Investigation</p><h2>Trace the path, then compare guarantees.</h2><p>Following the work through its pagination layer showed that sorted pagination was doing additional work even though the underlying database access already provided the ordering needed by this use case.</p><p>The abstraction’s name and general contract suggested a useful guarantee. The relevant question was narrower: did this access pattern need that guarantee twice?</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">What the abstraction was doing</p><h2>Database access already provided the ordering this path required.</h2><p>The sorted-pagination abstraction added sorting behavior on top of indexed access. For this path, that stronger behavior did not change the required output; it only added work before processing.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section visual"><p class="section-label">Before / after decision</p><CaseDiagram type="processing" /></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Decision</p><h2>Use the simpler pagination behavior where its contract is sufficient.</h2><p>After establishing that the stronger ordering behavior was redundant for this access pattern, I moved the path to regular pagination. The change removed work rather than shifting it elsewhere.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section visual compact"><p class="section-label">Validation and rollout</p><CaseDiagram type="rollout" /><p class="rollout-note">Output and content equivalence were validated before a canary rollout. Production metrics were monitored as the change expanded, and the result remained stable after rollout.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section result"><p class="section-label">Production result</p><div class="result-number"><span>~0.65s</span><i>→</i><strong>~0.15s</strong></div><p><b>~77% lower average production runtime</b>, observed after rollout and sustained through production monitoring.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Trade-off and lesson</p><h2>Evaluate abstractions against the guarantees a caller actually needs.</h2><p>Sorted pagination remains useful when its contract is required. Here, retaining it would have preserved unnecessary work. Generic abstractions have a cost, and extra guarantees should earn their place on a high-volume path.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose contribution"><p class="section-label">My contribution</p><h2>I followed the change from investigation through production evidence.</h2><p>I investigated the runtime, traced behavior into the pagination abstraction, validated output equivalence, implemented the change, and followed it through canary and production monitoring.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section status-section"><p class="section-label">Current status</p><div><h2>Shipped, monitored, and stable in production.</h2><p>The production path now uses regular pagination, with ~77% lower average runtime observed after rollout.</p></div></section>
  </>}
  {entry.id === 'shipping-new-format' && <>
    <section class="case-section glance"><p class="section-label">At a glance</p><div class="glance-grid"><div><small>Problem</small><p>A new format did not fit the assumptions of an existing serving pipeline.</p></div><div><small>My role</small><p>Serving-side design and implementation, guardrails, tests, and staged rollout.</p></div><div><small>Outcome</small><p>Launched successfully with no observed regression in the existing experience.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">The challenge</p><h2>The pipeline’s existing model encoded assumptions the new format did not share.</h2><p>Extending the serving path required more than adding another value. The system needed to recognize the new inventory semantics early enough to apply format-specific matching without changing normal video behavior.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose deadline"><p class="section-label">Constraint</p><h2>A cleaner model expanded the change beyond the first-release deadline.</h2><p>A dedicated model offered better long-term semantics, but it also pulled more of the stack into the initial delivery. The practical decision was to reduce first-release scope without closing the path to that cleaner architecture.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Architectural choice</p><h2>Classify early, carry meaning internally, and isolate the behavioral branch.</h2><p>The serving-side design introduced an early classification step and an internal semantic signal. Existing pipeline stages could remain shared, while pause-specific matching stayed in a clearly bounded branch.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section visual routing-section"><p class="section-label">Serving-side design</p><CaseDiagram type="routing" /></section>
    <section class="case-section guardrails"><div><p class="section-label">Guardrails and backward compatibility</p><h2>The default path remained the existing path.</h2><p>Specific behavior was introduced as a bounded exception, not allowed to leak into normal traffic.</p></div><ol><li><span>01</span><b>Classify correctly</b><p>Only explicitly detected pause inventory enters the new branch.</p></li><li><span>02</span><b>Match compatibly</b><p>Pause-specific matching remains isolated from existing video behavior.</p></li><li><span>03</span><b>Protect the contract</b><p>Tests, configuration gates, and staged exposure guard the new behavior and the path around it.</p></li></ol></section>
    <section class="case-section visual sequencing-section"><p class="section-label">Architectural sequencing</p><CaseDiagram type="sequencing" /><p class="sequence-thesis">The incremental release ordered the work. It did not reject the cleaner architecture.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose"><p class="section-label">Rollout</p><h2>Increase exposure only after the new branch proved coherent.</h2><CaseDiagram type="rollout-three" /><p class="rollout-note">Implementation and tests preceded a staged rollout, pilot validation, and launch.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose reflection"><p class="section-label">Trade-off and reflection</p><h2>Ship the smaller coherent change, while naming the debt it creates.</h2><p>The incremental model met the first-release goal while the dedicated model remained the cleaner long-term direction. I underestimated part of the implementation and migration cost elsewhere in the stack. Next time, I would make that cost explicit earlier and pair the incremental architecture with a clear migration owner and trigger. That does not make the sequencing decision wrong; it makes its follow-up obligations clearer.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section prose contribution"><p class="section-label">My contribution</p><h2>I led the serving-side implementation and the decisions within that boundary.</h2><p>I led classification and routing, isolated matching behavior, guardrails, tests, and staged rollout on the serving side. This was serving-side ownership within a broader cross-stack delivery, not a claim of redesigning the entire product alone.</p></section>
    <section class="case-section status-section"><p class="section-label">Current status</p><div><h2>Launched through a staged rollout.</h2><p>The new format shipped successfully, with no observed regression in the existing experience.</p></div></section>
  </>}
</CaseLayout>
